# Children of a brushed fill clip to the coverage its brush leaves, 192x72, three 56px squares on
# paper at y 36, each with one child drawn across it.
# Cell 1 (x 32), gold square with chip: the edge is bitten by flakes, and a dark band 72 wide
# crosses the square's left and right sides. The band stops at the flaked edge: each bite shows
# paper through the band, and the band never reaches past the chipped outline.
# Cell 2 (x 96), blue square with dry: horizontal streaks of paper break the blue. A dark bar
# crosses the square from top to bottom and is broken by the same streaks, so the bar shows only
# where blue is painted and the paper gaps run through it.
# Cell 3 (x 160), a faint gold square (alpha .25) with chip and a dark band like cell 1's: the
# square itself is pale, but the band inside it is as dark and opaque as in cell 1, and still
# stops at the flaked edge. Its flakes fall in other places than cell 1's.
# The .svg target uses profile:svg: a brushed node bakes into a raster tile, children included,
# so each cell looks like the PNG's.
@target brush/clip.png 192x72
@target brush/clip.svg 192x72 profile:svg

use std/brushes { dry chip }

rect 56 at:32,36 fill:p.gold.2 brush:chip(strength:.9 scale:2) name:chipped
  rect 72,14 fill:p.ink name:band
rect 56 at:96,36 fill:p.blue.2 brush:dry(strength:.6) name:streaked
  rect 12,72 fill:p.ink name:bar
rect 56 at:160,36 fill:#e8b33a40 brush:chip(strength:.9 scale:2) name:faint
  rect 72,14 fill:p.ink name:faint_band
